Import "../Physics.lingua"

Solve rolling constraints and check the required contact force (@physics-rotation-rolling: 0, is #chapter, #hephaestus-core "Separate-angular-momentum-exchange-from-kinetic-energy-conservation.lingua#r_00C38A235BF3E5DECE8E4ED34B") { r_0051818CAFA6E9742A1E62DF98
A rigid circular body can translate and rotate at once. Consider planar motion on a stationary straight surface with mass m>0, radius R>0 and central axial inertia I=κmR², κ>0. Choose positive translation along the surface to the right, and positive angular motion clockwise for forward rolling. This clockwise scalar convention is the negative of the positive-z convention in the opening lesson; change signs explicitly when comparing them. Let F be a signed force through the center along the surface, M a signed clockwise applied couple, f the signed contact force in the positive translation direction, and N>0 the normal force.

Translation and moment balance about the center give ma=F+f and Iα=M−Rf. Rolling without slip requires v=Rω and therefore a=Rα. The acceleration relation alone preserves rolling only when the initial velocities already satisfy v=Rω. Eliminating α and f gives a=(F+M/R)/[m(1+κ)] and f=(M/R−κF)/(1+κ). The required static force can point either way. The phrase friction opposes motion is too vague: static friction supplies a force that prevents relative slip, and its sign follows the coupled equations.

The proposed solution is admissible only if |f|≤μ_s N for the stated static-friction coefficient. Equality describes the limiting case, not every rolling state. If the inequality fails, these expressions are a rejected rolling candidate; a sliding/contact law and new kinematics are needed. They are not the actual accelerations of a slipping object. N itself must come from the normal force balance and contact assumptions; the model does not infer contact retention from a requested coefficient.

An original comparison takes m=2 kg, R=0.5 m, κ=1/2, N=20 N and μ_s=0.1. A center force F=6 N with M=0 yields a=2 m/s², α=4 rad/s² and f=−2 N, at the static limit. A pure couple M=3 N·m with F=0 gives the same formal a and α but f=+4 N. Its rolling candidate is inadmissible because only 2 N is available. Equal predicted acceleration does not establish equal contact requirements. The program returns both the candidate and its admissibility so this distinction cannot be hidden by one scalar output.

The contact point has tangential velocity v−Rω in this convention. Under ideal rolling its contact power is f(v−Rω)=0, although its separate contributions to translational and rotational energy are fv and −Rfω. They cancel; friction need not vanish. Consequently d[mv²/2+Iω²/2]/dt=Fv+Mω. This energy account is an independent check on the signs. Contact deformation, rolling resistance and a moving surface change the model and can make this zero-power simplification invalid.

For a downward incline angle β with no applied couple, F=mg sin β and N=mg cos β. Then a=g sin β/(1+κ), f=−κmg sin β/(1+κ) and μ_s≥κ tan β/(1+κ). With a uniform solid cylinder κ=1/2, the requirement is μ_s≥tan β/3. For sin β=3/5, cos β=4/5 and g=9.81 m/s², acceleration is 3.924 m/s² and minimum μ_s is 1/4. This is an original rational-angle extension of the symbolic cylinder example in [University Physics Volume 1 — OpenStax](../fluid-dynamics/sources/University-Physics-Volume-1.lingua#r_2W7QCEHZ45XDB3AWVG9NTV36F6), section 11.1, and the shared mechanical lesson. Uniform motion on a level surface with F=M=0 instead needs f=0.

**Work:** solve the three balance/constraint equations as a linear system before substituting the closed forms. Compute both example power accounts at v=3 m/s. Derive the incline result independently from loss of gravitational potential energy over a distance with rolling initially established. Explain why an instantaneous contact point should not casually be treated as a fixed material pivot throughout an interval. Distinguish a physically admissible rolling prediction from evidence that a real surface satisfies the assumed law.

**Continue:** [Separate angular momentum exchange from kinetic energy conservation](Separate-angular-momentum-exchange-from-kinetic-energy-conservation.lingua#r_00C38A235BF3E5DECE8E4ED34B).

**Return:** [Physics](../Physics.lingua#r_04A5CP8RTBERZEN4XCCDQ3T47W).
